  "content": {
    "id": "p19-q2-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "2",
    "prompt": "Show that there is exactly one Pythagorean triple of the form $(5,b,c)$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$(5,12,13)$.\n\nFactor $(c-b)(c+b)=25$; the only positive unequal factor pair is $(1,25)$.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}5^2+b^2&=c^2\\\\c^2-b^2&=25\\\\(c-b)(c+b)&=25\\end{align*}$$Since $c>b>0$, both factors are positive and $c+b>c-b$. The positive factor pairs of $25$ are $(1,25)$ and $(5,5)$. Equal factors would give $b=0$, so only $(1,25)$ is possible.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}c-b&=1\\\\c+b&=25\\\\2c&=26\\\\c&=13\\\\b&=12\\end{align*}$$The check $5^2+12^2=169=13^2$ confirms that $(5,12,13)$ is the unique triple.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 84
  },
